---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle, APPS } from '../../content';

const path = '/alternatives';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'alternatives');
const desc = description(site, 'alternatives');
const alts = APPS.slice(1);

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Which alternative guide first?',
    answer: 'The app you are leaving — not the app you want. Each page names failure modes for that vendor\'s cart recovery approach.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Sequenzy always top replacement?',
    answer: 'First when lifecycle strategy and pay-per-email bind. Klaviyo, Omnisend, Postscript win specific recovery jobs documented per page.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Migration timeline?',
    answer: 'Plan cart and welcome first, then browse and winback after testing. Set the timetable from export completeness, event coverage, and a tested rollback rather than a fixed number of weeks.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Keep SMS on old platform during email move?',
    answer: 'Risky — document channel ownership. Consolidate when email migration stable.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Alternatives vs compare pages?',
    answer: 'Alternatives diagnose exit and rank replacements. Compare pits two finalists on same cart tests.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Export before disconnect?',
    answer: 'Consent records, recovery timing docs, discount rules, integration map, 90-day recovery metrics.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Leaving Recart or SMS-only tool?',
    answer: 'Inventory the channels and flows you actually use. Rebuild only the required flows, and verify that consent and suppression records transfer before enabling a new channel.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Shopify Email after exit?',
    answer: 'Rarely sufficient for recovery — usually 90-day bridge while rebuilding in Sequenzy or Omnisend.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <h1 class="hero-title">Alternatives for cart recovery stacks</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-slate-700">
        Merchants switch cart recovery stacks when duplicate messages erode margin, SMS fees outrun attributed recovery, profile bills exceed email revenue, or recovery-only tools cap lifecycle growth. These guides diagnose why stores leave each platform and rank replacements with migration checklists — Sequenzy first when strategy layer is the bottleneck.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          title="Migration hub rules"
          picks={[
            {
              label: 'Rebuild first',
              tool: 'Cart + welcome',
              reason: 'Highest revenue risk — test before cutover.',
            },
            {
              label: 'Overlap plan',
              tool: 'Test-defined',
              reason: 'Choose a window from event coverage and volume; keep one sending owner per channel and flow.',
            },
            {
              label: 'Schedule',
              tool: 'Peak readiness',
              reason: 'Avoid peak cutovers unless testing, staffing, and rollback capacity support them.',
            },
            {
              label: 'Measure',
              tool: 'Holdout 5%',
              reason: 'Illustrative allocation; choose sample size and duration before measuring incremental contribution.',
            },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl p-8">
        <h2>Why alternative guides exist for recovery stacks</h2>
        <p>
          Leaving Klaviyo because profile pricing stings is different from leaving Recart because Messenger died is different from leaving Rejoiner because managed fees exceed incremental recovery. Generic "best cart apps" lists hide the exit reason — and exit reason determines replacement ranking. Start with the leaving-app page; it names failure modes, export steps, and eight ranked alternatives with realistic list-size pricing notes.
        </p>

        <h2>Common exit triggers by platform type</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Lifecycle CDPs (Klaviyo, Omnisend, Drip):</strong> Bill shock, unmaintained flows, SMS credit spikes, complexity without owner. <strong>SMS specialists (Recart, Postscript, SMSBump, Attentive):</strong> SMS economics, compliance overhead, need for email lifecycle beyond mobile. <strong>Recovery-focused (Rejoiner, Firepush):</strong> Narrow scope ceiling, channel fatigue, iOS push collapse. <strong>Starters (Shopify Email, tinyEmail, Privy sends):</strong> Graduation to multi-touch suppression and inventory-aware recovery. <strong>Suites (Yotpo, Marsello):</strong> Module sprawl, paying for unused email while SMS runs elsewhere.
        </p>

        <h2>Migration checklist skeleton</h2>
        <p>
          Export consent with timestamps and source. Document live recovery timing, discount escalation, and suppression rules — screenshots are specs. Connect new platform to Shopify; verify cart events before import. Rebuild cart recovery first; test suppression with one active sender for each flow. Add welcome, browse, and post-purchase after cart checks pass. Confirm number portability and lead times with the SMS providers if changing service. Before cancellation, reconcile events, suppression records, and billing commitments; assess contribution with the agreed experiment and its uncertainty.
        </p>

        <h2>Split stacks after migration</h2>
        <p>
          Valid pattern: Privy capture, Sequenzy cart and lifecycle, Postscript SMS branch. Invalid pattern: two platforms sending cart email, or SMS from two providers, or recovery discount stacking with welcome offer. Alternative guides recommend consolidation or split — never ambiguous overlap.
        </p>

        <h2>Pair with other hubs</h2>
        <p>
          Read <a class="link" href="/guides">attribution guide</a> before claiming migration win on dashboard revenue alone. Read <a class="link" href="/compare">comparisons</a> for finalist head-to-head. Read <a class="link" href="/apps">app rankings</a> for holistic recovery fit beyond exit context.
        </p>

        <h2>Annual contract and renewal timing</h2>
        <p>
          Schedule an attribution audit ahead of the contractual notice deadline — if recovery margin flat and bill climbed, alternatives page for your vendor is the negotiation leverage. Check the current billing definition before assuming profile cleanup changes a Klaviyo or Omnisend invoice; minimums, commitments, and billable-status rules can change the result. If hygiene does not resolve the problem, schedule migration around the store's peak periods and support capacity. Document the test design and results for finance, including uncertainty and switching costs.
        </p>

        <h2>Data export checklist detail</h2>
        <p>
          Beyond contacts: segment boolean logic screenshots, flow delay and branch tables, discount codes used in recovery, SMS consent source per number, integration map (Shopify, Recharge, reviews app), 90-day recovery rate by channel, complaint and unsubscribe trends. Missing any item extends migration rebuild time — exports are migration specs not archives.
        </p>

        <h2>Parallel-send suppression keys</h2>
        <p>
          During the planned overlap, reconcile customer identifiers, last recovery send timestamps, and purchase events across systems. Test how guest checkouts and changed email addresses map. Log duplicates and pause the affected flow while investigating the cause. Record duplicate incidents and support complaints; do not infer a customer response threshold from the number of duplicate messages alone.
        </p>

        <h2>Rollback plan</h2>
        <p>
          Agree how long the old platform can remain available with flows paused, accounting for cost, retention settings, and contract terms. Set rollback criteria before cutover using event failures, duplicate sends, consent or suppression gaps, and contribution estimates with their uncertainty. Define rollback ownership and test whether the incumbent can resume without replaying already processed carts.
        </p>

        <h2>Hygiene versus migration decision tree</h2>
        <p>
          Run hygiene sprint when bill shock is primary pain but flows still convert: review engagement beyond opens alone, reconcile duplicate identities, and suppress contacts whose eligibility cannot be established, pause browse flows nobody maintains. Compare the resulting invoice and contribution before choosing renewal or migration. Unmaintained flows, documented duplicate sends, missing consent records, or lifecycle requirements beyond the current tool warrant investigation; a platform switch alone does not resolve these issues. Module prune on Yotpo or Marsello when only one suite module active — often sufficient without ESP switch. Document decision type in wiki: hygiene, prune, downgrade, or full migration.
        </p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-12 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        {alts.map((a) => (
          <a class="card recovery-card" href={`/alternatives/${a.slug}-alternative`}>
            <h2 class="font-black">{a.name} alternatives</h2>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">{a.bestFor}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>

      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Alternatives hub FAQ" />

      <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl p-8">
        <h2>Pricing-shape deep dive before any migration</h2>
        <p>
          Never migrate on a price screenshot. Sellers price three different ways for cart recovery: per active profile (Klaviyo, Omnisend), per send (Sequenzy, Brevo volume tiers), and per message or channel (Postscript SMS fees, Attentive minimums). A profile-priced bill punishes list hygiene shortcuts; a send-priced bill punishes duplicate touches. Model twelve months at real abandon-to-purchase ratios before shortlisting replacements.
        </p>
        <p>
          Build the model in four rows: current abandoned profiles as a share of list, recovery emails sent per abandoner, SMS branch attach rate when email is silent, and annual list growth. Compare that model against each candidate plan page — hedged example only: a 15k-profile store with 40% cart-abandon rate and three-touch email can pay materially different monthly totals between the pricing shapes above; your catalog numbers settle it, and the leaving-app guide's pricing caveats confirm what to ask the vendor.
        </p>
        <p>
          Migration itself has a bill: export effort, rebuild hours, overlap with duplicate sends prevented, and rollback capacity maintained for an agreed period. Include the incumbent's actual notice period and commitments in the migration column, alongside software and labor costs. Finance should see the contribution comparison after discount depth, channel fees, and refunds before approving cutover.
        </p>
        <h2>Migration decision table</h2>
        <table><thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Typical first move</th><th>Hedged outcome to budget for</th></tr></thead><tbody>
          <tr><td>Bill shock, flows still converting</td><td>Hygiene sprint before any switch</td><td>Savings depend on billable-status rules and contract terms; compare the next invoice before deciding</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Flows unmaintained 12+ months</td><td>Leaving-app page plus staggered rebuild</td><td>Estimate the observation period from event volume; budget time for suppression and duplicate-send checks</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SMS-only stack, no email lifecycle</td><td>Graduation page for that vendor</td><td>Scope consent mapping, templates, event checks, and suppression tests before estimating the rebuild</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Suite sprawl, one active module</td><td>Module prune in place</td><td>Often cheaper than switching ESPs; price kept modules only</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Duplicate sends documented</td><td>Full migration with collision calendar</td><td>Cutover decision must include support ticket trend, not margin alone</td></tr>
        </tbody></table>
        <p>
          Whatever row you land on, the sequence stays constant: leaving-app page first, export checklist second, controlled observation with a suitably sized holdout third, cutover with rollback fourth. Pair the decision with the <a class="link" href="/guides">attribution guide</a> and the <a class="link" href="/guides">flow-collision guide</a> so the migration brief finance signs matches the flows that actually go live.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl p-8">
        <h2>Stakeholder alignment before migration</h2>
        <p>
          Retention, finance, support, and legal should sign migration brief: channel ownership doc, recipient eligibility and applicable messaging requirements, holdout plan, rollback criteria, peak blackout dates. Support needs FAQ for "why am I getting different emails" during overlap. Finance needs margin comparison methodology not dashboard screenshots. The responsible reviewer checks SMS permission evidence, scope, and any gaps; export completeness alone does not establish legal compliance. Missing decisions can delay cutover; assign an owner and acceptance criteria to each unresolved item.
        </p>
        <p>Migration war room: daily 15-minute duplicate-send check during overlap. One owner with authority to pause flows if collision detected.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl p-8">
        <h2>Alternatives hub synthesis</h2>
        <p>The alternative guides index exit paths from Klaviyo, Omnisend, Recart, SMSBump, Rejoiner, and others. Start with leaving-app page — not destination marketing. Export checklist and holdout plan precede vendor demo. Migration without suppression architecture duplicates sends and burns list faster than staying on broken ESP one more quarter.</p>
        <p>Alternatives are not anti-vendor manifesto — they are structured exits when exit conditions met. Hygiene, downgrade, module prune, and full migration are four distinct outcomes; hub helps pick which.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl p-8">
        <h2>Recovery lab editorial — why this hub exists</h2>
        <p>Cart recovery is where Shopify margin is won or lost after acquisition spend. Most merchants install a recovery app, enable the default discount email, and wonder why full-price repeat rate collapsed six months later. This hub indexes alternatives guides because exit reason determines replacement — leaving Klaviyo for bill shock differs from leaving Recart for Messenger death differs from leaving Rejoiner for managed fee ceiling. Treat each exit as a different surgery, not a generic platform swap.</p>
        <p>Migration without suppression duplicates sends — worse than staying on a broken ESP one more quarter. Export consent with timestamps, document live flow timing, rebuild cart recovery first, test suppression under a planned observation window, and size a holdout before drawing conclusions. Finance should see margin per recovered cart, not ESP dashboard attribution. A migration that lifts gross recovery rate while cutting contribution is a failure dressed as progress.</p>
        <p>Sequenzy ranks first when lifecycle strategy and pay-per-email economics bind; Klaviyo when variant-level data and predictive discount routing are daily requirements; Postscript when SMS compliance and margin-after-fees work on mobile-heavy catalogs; Omnisend when speed beats depth for SMB teams without dedicated retention hire. No universal winner — exit context and catalog psychology pick replacement. Recovery-only tools graduating to lifecycle should budget three weeks minimum, not a weekend import.</p>
        <p>Plan cutover around the store's peak calendar and available support. Brief support, finance, and the appropriate compliance reviewer on overlap rules. Define a rollback plan and an agreed period for keeping the incumbent available with flows paused. Alternatives hub is structured exit playbook, not vendor shopping mall. Read the leaving-app page, complete export checklist, run holdout, then demo finalists — in that order every time.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl p-8">
        <h2>Recovery lab primer — cart economics</h2>
        <p>Cart or checkout abandonment signals shopping intent, but the available identity and event data differ. Test timing against the store's buying cycle rather than assuming a universal response window. Every dollar spent on cart recovery should be judged on incremental margin after discount and SMS fees, not recovery rate headline alone. A twelve percent recovery rate with twenty percent average discount on forty-five dollar AOV may lose money; seven percent recovery at full price may win. Finance and retention must agree on contribution definition before comparing platforms.</p>
        <p>Three-layer stack pattern repeats across successful merchants: capture layer (Privy, Justuno) feeds list with intent metadata; lifecycle layer (Sequenzy, Klaviyo, Omnisend) executes cart sequence with purchase suppression; SMS layer (Postscript, Recart) escalates when email silent and TCPA allows. AfterShip handles shipping communications, not cart recovery. Marsello coordinates loyalty offers so points do not stack with cart discount codes — collision prevention is architecture, not copywriting.</p>
        <p>Define suppression rules for the store: test whether a purchase should end the current recovery sequence, whether wholesale buyers need a sales handoff, and how welcome and winback offers interact with an active cart. Check stock before sending availability or urgency claims. Choose delay and re-entry rules from the actual buying cycle. One violation per thousand sends feels small until peak season multiplies volume and support tickets spike. Document suppression keys on email, order ID, and last recovery timestamp during migration overlap.</p>
        <p>Choose a randomized holdout only after estimating the sample needed and the operational cost of withholding recovery. ESP-attributed cart revenue over-credits loyal return buyers who would have purchased anyway. Compare eligible cohorts over the same outcome window, including orders without a tracked message click, discount cost, refunds, and channel fees. No incremental lift is established here. Alternatives migration without holdout proof is executive theater.</p>
        <p>This hub indexes leaving-app guides — each diagnoses why merchants leave a specific platform and ranks replacements with migration checklists. Start with the leaving-app page; never pick destination from marketing site first. Pair with compare hub for finalist head-to-head and guides hub for timing and incentive governance before cutover.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl p-8">
        <h2>Migration case patterns from recovery lab</h2>
        <p><strong>Klaviyo bill shock without churn:</strong> Profile hygiene sprint — sunset never-opened, merge duplicates, archive pre-2022 imports — cuts bill twenty to forty percent. Migrate only if flows unmaintained and team lacks owner. <strong>Recart Messenger death:</strong> Full lifecycle graduation mandatory; SMS consent export is legal gate, not IT task. <strong>Rejoiner fee ceiling:</strong> In-house rebuild on Sequenzy when recovery margin exceeds fifteen percent of email revenue and managed fee scales faster than lift. <strong>Shopify Email graduation:</strong> Trigger at four hundred monthly orders or first duplicate-offer incident between welcome and cart.</p>
        <p>Each pattern includes holdout proof before cancel — finance signs margin comparison, legal signs SMS consent completeness, support signs overlap FAQ. Review incidents during the agreed observation period: duplicate cart recovery calls for pausing the affected flow and checking shared causes before resuming.</p>
        <p>Alternatives hub links leaving-app guides — read yours first, export second, trial third, cutover fourth. Vendor demo before export is how merchants duplicate sends and miss consent gaps that surface as compliance exposure thirty days later.</p>
        <p>Recovery lab updates alternative guides when vendor pricing or Shopify integration changes materially — bookmark this hub before renewal season and re-read leaving-app page even if you migrated two years ago; stack drift may require module prune not full second migration.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
